## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of cross-account access, data isolation, and automation in a multi-tenant SaaS architecture. It probes patterns beyond PrivateLink, focusing on RAM sharing and per-tenant bucket access controls.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles and trust policies
- AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) sharing
- S3 Access Points and bucket policies
- Organizations SCPs for guardrails
- Onboarding/offboarding automation and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Trust policy for cross-account role (AnalyticsAccount assumes TenantRole)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ANALYTICS_ACCOUNT_ID:root"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses design of scalable, compliant pipelines with immutability, latency, DR, and cost in AWS.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Firehose, S3, Object Lock, SSE-KMS
- Glue Streaming, Lambda, DynamoDB state
- DLQ, cross-region replication, CloudWatch

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent Lambda sketch
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
exports.handler = async (evt) => {
  const key = { id: evt.id };
  const exists = await ddb.get({TableName:'Processed', Key:key}).promise();
  if (exists.Item) return;
  await ddb.put({TableName:'Processed', Item:{id: evt.id, t: Date.now()}}).promise();
  // enrich data
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate data quality and schema evolution across regions?
- How do you handle late-arriving data and reprocessing without duplicating work?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross‑account backup/restore patterns, data residency, encryption, and auditable workflows. Demonstrates handling of least privilege, cross-account trusts, and automated testing.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles and trust policies
- KMS key grants per tenant
- S3 bucket policies and cross-account access
- DR testing and auditing

